<https://citekg.org/resource/citation/8a9d22295eb28701/27> <http://purl.org/spar/c4o/hasContent> "made the system applicable to that real-life scenario. That reasoning process under uncertainty was performed with a Bayesian inference engine applying a causal model of faults, which relates the cause of the fault and their symptoms. However, as that reasoning process was centralised in one single agent, the complexity of that Bayesian model increased when new systems and services had to be diagnosed by MAS, which made the system robustness decrease, and the causal model maintainability was more costly. The Bayesian reasoning technique is widely used for fault diagnosis in the literature [26,27] for different kinds of networks, such as high-speed rail networks [cite], wireless sensor networks [cite],or optical networks [cite]. Consequently, some distributed reasoning techniques were explored to solve that scalability isues. Our first attempt was to apply one of the existing techniques for distributed Bayesian inference [cite], such as Distributed Perception Network (DPN) [cite] or Multiple Sectioned Bayesian Network (MSBN) [cite]. However, some requirements for applying those techniques in the considered scenario, i.e., a dynamic and complex environment such as a telecommunication ne"@en .
